    "description": "Iranian esports includes organized qualification, game-center competition, and participation in international events. Documented examples show different pathways for team games and individual contests, alongside international success in football simulation.",
    "tags": ["Esports", "Competitive gaming", "National teams", "eFootball", "Gaming communities"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:57:37Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Esports in Iran: Players and Qualification | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iranian esports through documented national qualifiers, international competition, and the distinction between institutional aims and results."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "overview",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From playing a game to organized competition",
        "content": "Esports means organized competition in video games. For Iran, documented examples include national selection in team-based games and individual fighting games, followed by opportunities to compete internationally. These formats require different competitive arrangements: a team must qualify collectively, while an individual contestant advances through a personal bracket. An IESF account of Iranian qualification provides a useful historical view of those distinctions. [s1]\n\nInstitutional representation is another part of the picture. The Asian Electronic Sports Federation, or AESF, lists an Iran Esports Association and describes its aims as developing the community, holding national championships, and sending national teams abroad. This establishes the regional federation’s published description of the association; it does not independently demonstrate how comprehensively those aims have been achieved. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qualification",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A documented qualification pathway",
        "content": "In September 2018, the International Esports Federation, or IESF, reported Iranian qualifiers for Counter-Strike: Global Offensive, League of Legends, and Tekken. Its account described online and offline stages for Counter-Strike, separate preliminary brackets for League of Legends, and a game-center event for Tekken. Junior and Infinity game centers appeared as physical competition venues. The report demonstrates that the pathway combined remote participation with organized, in-person play. [s1]\n\nThe same account distinguished winning qualification from being able to travel. Organizers mentioned military-service-related travel difficulties and possible replacement of selected players. That is a dated statement about the delegation’s circumstances, not a complete explanation of current travel rules. It nevertheless illustrates an important distinction: a competitive result and an international appearance are separate events, and selection alone does not establish attendance. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "international",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "International results need a precise frame",
        "content": "Iranian player Hassan Pajani won the eFootball title at the IESF championship in Iași, Romania, in September 2023, according to specialist publication L’Académie eFootball. Its report identifies his final opponent as Japan’s Tsubasa “Leva” Aihara. eFootball is a football simulation: the competitive activity takes place through the video game rather than on a football pitch. [s3]\n\nThe result belongs to a particular game and event. IESF’s own championship review describes a broader program spanning football simulation, fighting, shooting, multiplayer battle-arena, and mobile games. Its overall country award went to the Philippines. A title won by an Iranian player therefore should not be restated as Iran winning the entire championship or leading every esports discipline. The distinction helps readers understand both the significance and the limits of an individual achievement. [s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Understanding the scene beyond a medal",
        "content": "These records reveal several layers of Iranian esports: places where contestants gather, systems that select representatives, institutions that describe development goals, and players who achieve international results. They do not establish how many people earn a living from competition, how participation varies between provinces, or how accessible organized play is to women and newcomers. Those questions require evidence beyond a federation directory or a successful tournament appearance.\n\nFor an international reader, the most useful starting point is therefore the game, the organizer, and the date. Naming all three makes an achievement intelligible and prevents different competitions from being treated as interchangeable.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "historical-pathway",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Selected Iranian qualification routes documented in 2018",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Counter-Strike online stage\"] -->|\"Qualified teams advanced\"| B[\"Counter-Strike offline stage\"]\n  B -->|\"Determined winner\"| C[\"Counter-Strike qualifier champion\"]\n  D[\"League of Legends preliminary brackets\"] -->|\"Leading teams advanced\"| E[\"League of Legends offline final\"]\n  E -->|\"Determined winner\"| F[\"League of Legends qualifier champion\"]\n  G[\"Tekken game-center qualifier\"] -->|\"Determined winner\"| H[\"Tekken qualifier champion\"]\n```",
        "summary": "These separate routes summarize a historical qualification report. The arrows indicate advancement or determination of a winner; they do not establish that every winner subsequently traveled to the international event.",
        "as_of_date": "2018-09-03",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
